00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:45.400
Like write some realistic tasks that you want the agent to do that

251
00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:48.920
requires usage of the context that you've created,

252
00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:53.800
and then write a little scoring rubric for what a good solution to that problem

253
00:12:53.800 --> 00:12:54.760
looks like.

254
00:12:54.760 --> 00:12:56.600
The reason I say write a good scoring rubric

255
00:12:56.600 --> 00:13:00.400
and not something like write a bunch of unit tests, is twofold.

256
00:13:00.760 --> 00:13:03.920
The first is that, unit tests are

257
00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:07.120
really obnoxious to write, and they take a long time

258
00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:09.200
and you will quickly find that you just don't do it.

259
00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:12.480
If you have to create example projects and test suites

260
00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:15.680
for every single piece of context that you want to verify.

261
00:13:16.760 --> 00:13:20.280
More importantly, though, I'm sure many of you have seen that agents

262
00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:23.520
do unspeakable things to get unit tests to pass.

263
00:13:24.280 --> 00:13:27.040
And so functional correctness is not the only thing that you're measuring,

264
00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:28.560
especially for context.

265
00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:33.040
A lot of times you want to know, was idiomatic code written or did it use

266
00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:37.360
the library actually wanted it to use instead of implementing its own solution.

267
00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:41.160
And there's really no way to measure this with unit tests.

268
00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:45.920
It's much better to do more energetic review or less judge.

269
00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:49.160
So what you're going to want to do, I see somebody taking photos,

270
00:13:49.160 --> 00:13:50.640
so I'll let them take the photo.

